to Latin America now we're another year  of serious unrest could lie ahead now in  2019 a wave of protests broke out across  the continent so let's take a look at  some of the countries affected well  Venezuela remains in the grip of an  economic crisis a year ago opposition  leader Juan Guido claimed the presidency  from Nicolas Maduro who's accused of  election fraud and dictatorial rule now  meanwhile Argentina while it is in a  deep recession and unable to pay its  foreign debt or more than one hundred  billion dollars in neighboring Bolivia  President Evo Morales was pushed from  power in November while in Chile and  Colombia protesters are demanding better  governance and solutions to economic  inequality well for more on this now  we're joined by DWS nicole cleric in the  Colombian capital Bogota  good to see you : have you noticed any  similarities in the situations between  various South American countries such as  Colombia such as Chile for example  giving rise to that unrest although at  their core these movements are of course  very different there are significant  similarities these are grassroots  movement started by young people by  students who are fed up of a system that  they inherited from a generation that  was perhaps too scared to speak up for  themselves but is now embracing the  movement they're mostly organizing on  social media and as globalized citizens  they are of course looking at other  countries for inspiration and solidarity  another parallel there is between  especially Chile and Colombia is the  amount of demands that protesters have  this is not a protest movement against a  president a politician a single law this  is against the system here in Colombia  for example demands range from a pension  reform to a full implementation of the  peace agreement with the FARC a better  protection for indigenous people and  many people are also asking for a health  care reform and I talked to some people  here in Bogota to find out why Juan  Manuel loves working at the hospital he  likes to take his time with patients  making sure they feel safe and that  understand their conditions and  treatments but doing the job with such  dedication has become increasingly  difficult for many people working in  Colombian healthcare News una de cada  cuatro  one out of four health care  professionals are unhappy se siente in  feliz una los vecinos  one of our system's shortcomings is the  pay that physicians receive some of them  have to work two or three jobs to make  ends meet but overworked and underpaid  doctors and nurses aren't the only ones  feeling let down by Colombia's health  care system even in the capital Bogota  where there are plenty of hospitals and  clinics patients are finding it  impossible to get even the most urgent  appointments da da da I called the  hospital every day but they don't have  time for me I tell them please I suffer  from multiple sclerosis I need special  treatment  they never have time they give you an  appointment after one or even three  months it's horrible the health care  service here is awful such problems are  related to structural weaknesses  Colombia's health care system is  dominated by private insurance companies  numerous of these intermediaries between  patients and doctors have hit the  headlines for embezzlement and  corruption the president of Bogota  Medical Association says it was their  greed that caused the system to implode  the less services these companies  provide the more money they make so the  insurance company denies treatments all  takes a long time to authorize them  allowing the patient's condition to  deteriorate were they even refused to  cover therapy altogether necessary  treatments denied without explanation it  almost destroyed this young woman's  family her brother had alarming symptoms  but his doctors weren't allowed to run  the tests they needed to diagnose him it  took his insurance companies seven  months to authorize a cat scan from  January until August when they finally  got the results they saw  he had a brain tumor  when we found out he had a tumor he'd  already lost his hearing and suffered  irreversible brain damage  Mariana is a nurse herself and has been  participating in the protests suffering  from the healthcare systems shortcomings  as a patient and a professional she  urges her fellow Colombians to keep  fighting for change I'm confident there  will be change someday maybe not exactly  how we want it to be that would be  perfect and I don't believe that's gonna  happen but some kind of change no matter  how small for the time being it doesn't  look like the government will heed  people's calls but in the waiting room  and on the streets Colombians have run  out of patience straight back over to  our correspondent Nicole scalish there  in Bogota  Nicole people are clearly running out of  patience are there leaders who can  represent them in their struggle well we  have to bear in mind that this is a  protest against the entire political  class so there's not one opposition  figure that could step up and represent  demonstrate and the protesters claims  here in Colombia the strike committee  made up of several unions has been  trying to establish talks with the  government but these have failed over  and over again because the committee  wants these talks to be branded as  negotiations and that is something the  government doesn't want to give in to so  here semantics are getting into the way  of finding a solution and Chile the  situation is different it's a completely  leaderless movement people are  organizing in in groups according to  where they live or where they're their  interests lie what they care most about  but there's not one person or group that  can represent the entire movement some  people say that this is just proof of  how democratic these protests are but it  could ultimately lead to a weakening of  the entire movement  so clearly diverse strands across the  continent but nevertheless what can  people in these countries expect in 2020  it's hard to say seeing the commitment  of people out in the streets it's  difficult to believe that this could  just fizzle out it is still a great  opportunity for embattled leaders like  Sebastian Pinera like even dukkha here  in Colombia to truly listen to the  people to show commitment and honesty  and finding a solution to this and  changed the face of their countries  forever but if the past couple of months  are anything to go by this is very  unlikely to happen  all right I'll correspondent Nicola  foolish monitoring that wave of unrest  for us thanks a lot Nicole good to speak  to you
 

